14539	2rLvCcTPw7Y	22760	Space Tug	Excerpt: "Joe Kenmore heard the airlock close with a sickening wheeze and then a clank. In desperation he turned toward Haney. "My God, we've been locked out!" Through the transparent domes of their space helmets, Joe could see a look of horror and disbelief pass across Haney's face. But it was true! Joe and his crew were locked out of the Space Platform. Four thousand miles below circled the Earth. Under Joe's feet rested the solid steel hull of his home in outer space. But without tools there was no hope of getting back inside. Joe looked at his oxygen meter. It registered thirty minutes to live."\n\nCategories: Fiction	https://i.ytimg.com/vi/2rLvCcTPw7Y/hqdefault.jpg	2025-11-17 00:37:27.969552+00	176	\N
14540	GGOOdzSplqc	18718	Operation Terror	"He speaks with a directness of the heart . . . with a craftsmanship and consistency that warrant the nickname he was given: the Dean of SF." -- The Encyclopedia of Science Fiction "Murray Leinster" was the pen name of William Fitzgerald Jenkins (1896-1975) - an author whose career spanned the first six decades of the 20th Century. From mystery and adventure stories in the earliest years to science fiction in his later years, he worked steadily and at a highly professional level of craftsmanship longer than most writers of his generation. He won a Hugo Award in 1956 for his novelet "Exploration Team," and in 1995 the Sidewise Award for Alternate History took its name from his classic story, "Sidewise in Time." His last original work appeared in 1967. "Operation Terror," first published in 1962, is a novel of alien invasion, as inhuman monsters land and their conquest of Earth begins . . .\n\nCategories: Fiction	https://i.ytimg.com/vi/GGOOdzSplqc/hqdefault.jpg	2025-11-17 00:37:30.795385+00	176	\N
14541	XnsqcGuXucE	19470	Men into space: Exploring Outer Space: Classic Tales of Human Adventure in the Cosmos	In "Men into Space," Murray Leinster embarks on a visionary journey that intertwines science fiction with a palpable sense of realism rooted in the burgeoning Space Age. The narrative follows a group of astronauts as they venture into the cosmos, tackling not only the physical challenges of space travel but also the psychological and moral dilemmas that accompany such profound exploration. Leinster employs a vivid, accessible style that combines technical ingenuity with compelling storytelling, reflecting the optimism and anxiety of a mid-20th century society on the cusp of interstellar exploration, thus situating the novel within a crucial literary context where science fiction began to reflect real-world scientific advancements. Murray Leinster, a prolific writer often regarded as one of the forefathers of American science fiction, drew on his extensive background in early 20th-century science and technology to inform his narratives. With a career spanning several decades, he understood the potential of space travel not just as a speculative venture but as a probable future, influencing his richly detailed portrayals of both technology and human complexity. His work is often characterized by a deep belief in human ingenuity, showcasing the overarching themes found in "Men into Space." I highly recommend "Men into Space" to both enthusiasts of classic science fiction and modern readers interested in the thematic evolution of space exploration literature. Murray Leinster'Äôs imaginative foresight and eloquent prose invite readers to not only grapple with the mysteries of the universe but also to reflect on the essence of humanity in the face of unparalleled unknowns.\n\nCategories: Fiction	https://i.ytimg.com/vi/XnsqcGuXucE/hqdefault.jpg	2025-11-17 00:37:34.018916+00	176	\N
14551	7iR98GOMloQ	22279	The Wailing Asteroid	This carefully crafted ebook: "The Wailing Asteroid" is formatted for your eReader with a functional and detailed table of contents. "The Wailing Asteroid" is a science-fiction novel written by Murray Leinster. It presents the theme of the lone inventor who saves Earth from cosmic danger and introduces Humanity to an unknown civilization. The novel was adapted for film as "The Terrornauts" in 1967.\n\nCategories: Young Adult Fiction	https://i.ytimg.com/vi/7iR98GOMloQ/hqdefault.jpg	2025-11-17 00:38:18.203171+00	176	\N
14542	UmWu_LGRoUU	20175	Creatures of the Abyss: Or, The Listeners	Murray Leinster was a prolific and outstanding writer of Science fiction. He was born William Fitzgerald Jenkins on June 16, 1896 in Norfolk, Virginia and finished his formal education at the age of 13. With the loss of his father's job and subsequent downturn in the family's fortunes, Leinster would not be able to pursue the career in chemistry that he had longed for. But he would go on to another significant achievement - publishing more than 1, 500 short stories, novellas, and novels in his lifetime. As well as science fiction, Leinster wrote love stories, murder mysteries, adventure stories, westerns, fantasy, television and film scripts, and mainstream fiction. Leinster wrote variously over the years as Will F. Jenkins (mostly for mainstream magazines such as Colliers or The Saturday Evening Post), Murray Leinster (mostly for sci-fi), William F. Jenkins, William Fitzgerald, and even as Louisa Carter Lee for romance novels and potboilers. Leinster was definitely a renaissance man of words and ideas. But he is remembered for his remarkable prescience and vision in the sci-fi genre, especially around innovations in science and communications technologies.\n\nCategories: Underwater exploration	https://i.ytimg.com/vi/UmWu_LGRoUU/hqdefault.jpg	2025-11-17 00:37:37.086661+00	176	\N
